    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-39954-x",
    "abstract": "This study aimed to model the impact of climate-smart agricultural (CSA) practices on soil\u2012water balance, water use efficiency (WUE<sub>ET</sub>), and wheat yield in the face of climate change. The AquaCrop version 7.1 model was used to estimate the water balance and yield under the baseline (1981-2010) and future (2050s, RCP4.5) climate scenarios. We evaluated five CSA practices, varying in tillage, residue management, and water management, based on experiments conducted in 2020 and 2021. Observed data on wheat (Triticum aestivum L.) grain yield and surface runoff were used for model calibration (2020) and evaluation (2021). The model was evaluated using four performance indicators and found to be robust. The treatments included farmers' conventional practices (CPs), soil bunds (SBs), crop residues (CRs), integrated conservation practices (ICPs), and berken plows (BPs). The results show that climate change is likely to reduce grain yield and WUE<sub>ET</sub> under CP by 1% and 16.3%, respectively, by 2050 compared to the current 2021 period. All CSA practices studied increased grain yield and WUE<sub>ET</sub> over the CP in both periods. Under future climates, ICP showed a greater relative grain yield (Y = 4.51 t/ha), water use efficiency (WUE<sub>ET</sub> = 1.32 kg m<sup>3</sup>), and other soil water balances, followed by CR, BP, and SB over CP. Overall, ICP has shown tremendous potential for climate change adaptation among the other CSA practices tested. Therefore, adaptation to future climate conditions must integrate different practices, and the novel ICP can be promoted as a climate-smart practice in similar farming systems and agro-ecological settings.",
    "field": "Climate-smart agriculture",
    "problem": "This paper addresses the lack of integrated research on how combining climate-smart agricultural practices can improve water use efficiency and wheat yields in central Ethiopia, where conventional farming exacerbates crop failure and vulnerability to climate change through significant non-productive water loss and soil degradation.",
    "method": "This study uses the AquaCrop model, calibrated with field data, to simulate the impacts of five climate-smart agricultural practices on wheat yield and soil-water balance under baseline and future climate change scenarios in central Ethiopia.",
    "task": "The task is to model the impact of climate-smart agricultural practices on soil-water balance, water use efficiency, and wheat yield under future climate change scenarios in central Ethiopia using the AquaCrop model.",
